"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 4.6 and the surgical finding of positive margins. The pathological grade was Gleason 4+3 (ISUP group 3), which contributes to risk assessment. A countervailing finding is the absence of seminal vesicle invasion and lymphovascular invasion. Notably, there were no lymph nodes removed, meaning nodal status is unknown, which widens uncertainty regarding prognosis. Furthermore, the biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows a discrepancy, with the biopsy identifying Gleason 3+3 (ISUP group 1) versus the prostatectomy specimen showing higher grade disease. The absence of postoperative PSA and follow-up data limits prognostic certainty. Future information, particularly serial postoperative PSA measurements and their trajectory, would significantly revise this assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 37.67–87.91 months; its coverage has not been validated."
